<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Entitlements for every signed file that is NOT the top-level app bundle —
  electron-builder's default path for this file, applied automatically by
  its MacTargetHelper.getOptionsForFile to anything that isn't the app
  itself and isn't under Library/LoginItems. Traced in
  app-builder-lib/out/mac/MacTargetHelper.js: this is NOT limited to
  Electron's helper .app bundles (GPU/Renderer/Plugin/normal) — it is also
  what gets applied to every Mach-O under Resources/server (the claude
  binary, and the .node native modules), since those are signed in the same
  pass and are equally "not the top-level app".

  That matters concretely: the spawned `claude` binary is itself a Node/V8
  process. It needs the same allow-jit / allow-unsigned-executable-memory
  entitlements the main process needs, for the same reason (its own V8
  instance marks pages executable at runtime) — this file is what supplies
  them to it, not entitlements.mac.plist (which only ever applies to the
  top-level bundle).

  Deliberately NOT included here: com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation.
  entitlements.mac.plist DOES carry it as of 2026-08-13 — read that file for
  the measured failure that forced it. The split is intentional, so do not
  "make the two files consistent" without re-reading this paragraph.

  The entitlement is needed by exactly one process: the one that loads native
  `.node` modules out of the USER'S project (their vite/rollup/fsevents), and
  that is the top-level executable, since `process.execPath` under
  ELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE is `Contents/MacOS/Desde` (desktop/child.ts:121,
  main.ts:579). Nothing signed with THIS file loads a foreign-signed library:
  the Electron helper apps load only Electron's own frameworks, and the
  binaries under Resources/server (the `claude` binary, our two payload
  `.node` files) are all re-signed with Mo's identity in the same pass — same
  Team ID, so library validation is satisfied without weakening anything.

  Keeping it off here is not caution for its own sake. It means a compromised
  helper or the spawned `claude` binary still cannot map a dylib from an
  arbitrary signer, which is the whole protection the top-level process has
  to give up to do its job.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
  <dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
    <true/>
  </dict>
</plist>
